jordan: Customer (AcmePayments) opened a ticket: while streaming in JSON/structured mode, their client sees a truncated object after a keepalive/heartbeat and fails to parse. They observed a reconnect shortly after and the next connection contains the tail of the previous JSON object (duplicate partial) which breaks strict parsers. Attaching minimal capture below.

jordan: raw SSE capture (shortened):
```
data: {"id":"r1", "choices":[{"delta":{"content":"Hello"}}]}

data: {"id":"r1", "choices":[{"delta":{"content":" world"}}]}

data: ---HEARTBEAT---

[data: connection reset / TCP FIN from client]

# new TCP connection (same session)
data: {"id":"r1", "choices":[{"delta":{"content":"!"}}]}
```

jordan: In the failing case the client saw: the last chunk from first connection ended mid-object (no trailing `}`) and after reconnect the first bytes were the remaining suffix of that object, but because the client treats each SSE "data:" line as separate JSON blob it tried to parse a partially-closed JSON and error'd.

sam: Asking a few quick things:
- session id from request headers (x-session-id)
- edge region / carrier (customer says eu-west-1 via Cloudflare)
- client language (node 18 fetch, using `eventsource` polyfill)
- whether they had `Accept: text/event-stream` + `structured: json` header

sam: can you paste the exact request/response headers and a short curl repro? also is there any proxy between client and us?

jordan: Provided logs and curl snippet:
```
curl -N -H "Accept: text/event-stream" \n  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \n  -H "X-Session-Id: 86a2c9" \n  "https://api.redwood.example/v1/streams?model=redwood-medium&mode=structured"
```

jordan: customer says CF Worker sits at edge that injects a periodic keepalive `data: 
` to keep connections alive and sometimes the worker rewrites the SSE frames. They suspect the worker coalesces writes in a way that splits JSON boundaries.

maria: From runtime side: this looks like a boundary-handling bug in our SSE framing layer when intermediate keepalives are present. Two likely failure modes:
1) our server flushes partial JSON as `data:` chunks (we usually stream tokens, not full objects) and a proxy inserts an extra `data:` keepalive between bytes of a single JSON event, splitting the logical JSON across SSE records; many strict parsers expect one event==one well-formed JSON and will fail.
2) on reconnect, the client library replays buffered bytes or the edge left a small tail in the TCP pipeline that ends up as a new `data:` event containing a suffix.

maria: Short-term mitigations:
- recommend customer disable structured streaming (use newline-delimited JSON or no structured mode) until we patch.
- client-side tolerant parsing: accumulate SSE `data:` lines until a full JSON object parse succeeds, rather than parsing each line independently.

maria: Longer-term fixes we should consider:
- switch SSE encoder to ensure each logical JSON is emitted as a single atomic SSE event (prefer `event: json` + single `data:` containing the whole object) or explicitly include a per-event `event: seq` with a sequence id and `end: true` marker.
- make parser resilient: buffer across `data:` frames and only attempt parse when braces match / JSON.parse completes.

qa_jun: I was able to reproduce locally by inserting a tiny worker that writes a keepalive between writes: the sequence of writes becomes: first half of JSON -> "data: 
" -> second half of JSON. Node eventsource polyfill treats each `data:` as a separate message and we see parse errors. Repro steps:
```
# CF Worker pseudo-code that triggers it
addEventListener('fetch', event => {
  const upstream = fetch(event.request);
  const stream = new ReadableStream({
    start(controller) {
      // write first chunk of SSE event
      controller.enqueue(encode('data: {"foo":"part1')));
      // flush keepalive from worker
      controller.enqueue(encode('data: \n\n'));
      // write second half
      controller.enqueue(encode('data: part2}\n\n'));
      controller.close();
    }
  });
  return event.respondWith(new Response(stream, {headers: {'Content-Type':'text/event-stream'}}));
});
```

qa_jun: Filing a small repro harness in qa infra and attaching to the ticket.

maria: For the server patch: I'll land a conservative change in the SSE emitter to ensure we never split a single JSON object across multiple `data:` lines. That should be low-risk. I'll also add a feature flag to enable `sse-seq` which adds `event: seq` numbers so downstream clients can detect missing parts.

eng-bot: Created ticket REDWOOD-8764 for engineering (link: https://tracker.redwood.ai/REDWOOD-8764). Priority set to P2 initially; escalate to P1 if customer confirms production break and cannot work around.

support-bot: Customer updated: they are hitting this in prod, parsing failures on ~3% of streams. Marking this as P1 and requesting dedicated eng oncall. Workaround suggested to customer: use a resilient client SSE collector (example attached) or switch off structured streaming for now. :warning: \n
sam: I'll coordinate with customer to confirm whether they can roll the CF worker change (preferable) or temporarily switch mode. Also requesting edge packet captures and the worker source for root cause.

jordan: attaching customer's edge trace and minimal CF worker snippet to the ticket. Will update thread once eng lands quick patch. :thumbsup:
